From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:46:41 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] platform/x86: int3472: Set the DSM clock frequency from the
 SSDB

When the INT3472 has no clock enable GPIO, the sensor clock is switched
through the 82c0d13a-78c5-4244-9bb1-eb8b539a8d11 _DSM with the arguments
{ clock source, enable, frequency }. The driver always passes 1 as the
third argument, while it reports the SSDB mclkspeed as the clock rate.

On Alder Lake the third argument is the frequency select bit of the PCH
IMGCLKOUT control register: the DSDT of the Microsoft Surface Pro 9
writes it unchanged into bit 0 of \_SB.ICLK.CLKn (CLKF), and coreboot's
Alder Lake camera_clock_ctl.asl documents that bit as 0 = 24 MHz,
1 = 19.2 MHz.

The Surface Pro 9 IR sensor (SMO55F0) declares a 24 MHz clock in its
SSDB, so it currently gets 19.2 MHz while the clock framework reports
24 MHz. Pass 0 when the SSDB asks for 24 MHz and keep 1 otherwise, so
sensors declaring 19.2 MHz are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/platform/x86/intel/int3472/clk_and_regulator.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/platform/x86/intel/int3472/clk_and_regulator.c b/drivers/platform/x86/intel/int3472/clk_and_regulator.c
index 9e052b164..8cf3a02d4 100644
--- a/drivers/platform/x86/intel/int3472/clk_and_regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/intel/int3472/clk_and_regulator.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/platform_data/x86/int3472.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/driver.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/units.h>
 
 /*
  * 82c0d13a-78c5-4244-9bb1-eb8b539a8d11
@@ -19,6 +20,10 @@ static const guid_t img_clk_guid =
 	GUID_INIT(0x82c0d13a, 0x78c5, 0x4244,
 		  0x9b, 0xb1, 0xeb, 0x8b, 0x53, 0x9a, 0x8d, 0x11);
 
+/* Third argument of the clock _DSM: IMGCLKOUT frequency select bit */
+#define INT3472_IMGCLK_FREQ_24MHZ	0
+#define INT3472_IMGCLK_FREQ_19_2MHZ	1
+
 static void skl_int3472_enable_clk(struct int3472_clock *clk, int enable)
 {
 	struct int3472_discrete_device *int3472 = to_int3472_device(clk);
@@ -35,7 +40,9 @@ static void skl_int3472_enable_clk(struct int3472_clock *clk, int enable)
 	args[1].integer.type = ACPI_TYPE_INTEGER;
 	args[1].integer.value = enable;
 	args[2].integer.type = ACPI_TYPE_INTEGER;
-	args[2].integer.value = 1;
+	args[2].integer.value = clk->frequency == 24 * HZ_PER_MHZ ?
+				INT3472_IMGCLK_FREQ_24MHZ :
+				INT3472_IMGCLK_FREQ_19_2MHZ;
 
 	argv4.type = ACPI_TYPE_PACKAGE;
 	argv4.package.count = 3;
